{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 39, :comments ["<b>And the name of his wife was Meheitabel, daughter of Matred, daughter of Mei-Zahav.</b> That is, the daughter of a rich man who was occupied all his days with refining gold, and the gold flowed in his house like water." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: 'And these are the kings who reigned in the land of Edom' — these chieftains (alufim) were the descendants of Esav according to the plain sense of the verse, and this is the opinion of our Rabbis of blessed memory, who said: all of them — the verse speaks of those who issued from Esav's thigh, and they were idolaters and men of harlotry and great wealth, both the kings and the chieftains. And this is what they said in Bereshit Rabbah: 'And the name of his wife was Meheitabel' — they used to adorn divinity, that is, they would adorn themselves for idols. 'Daughter of Matred' — after they adorned her for one husband, they would remove her from that husband. 'Daughter of Mei-Zahav' — from those who say in Aramaic, 'what is gold (dahava), what is silver (kesafa)?' — end quote. This teaches that the descendants of Esav were wicked. There are also in them allusions to the seed of Esav throughout the generations, as they said in the Midrash: 'Aluf Magdiel' — this is Rome. And in Pirkei de-Rabbi Eliezer, chapter 38: 'In reward that he emptied his vessels for the sake of Jacob his brother, He gave him a hundred provinces from Se'ir to Magdiel, and Magdiel is Rome, as it is said: Aluf Magdiel, Aluf Eiram' — end quote. And this is what Daniel prophesied concerning it (Daniel 11): 'ויתרומם ויתגדל על כל אל.' Therefore it is mentioned by the name Magdiel, from the language of greatness and dominion great, and it is the tenth among these chieftains, just as Daniel mentioned (Daniel 7): 'וקרניא עשר מנה מלכותה עשרה מלכין יקומון' and so forth. The matter is that ten kings are destined to reign over Edom, and the tenth of them will reign over Rome, and from Rome his kingdom will spread over the entire world. And this nation — Scripture refers to its matter obscurely and did not explicitly tell us its name. And so we find that the prophets always mention the greatness of its dominion but do not mention it by name. Thus we find in Daniel (7:4–6) that he compared one kingdom to a lion, the second to a bear, and the third to a leopard, but did not mention the name of the fourth beast — only that he described it as 'דחילא ואמתני ותקיפא יתרא ושנין די פרזל לה אכלה ומדקה ושארא ברגלה רפסה.' And so Isaiah, of blessed memory, mentioned (Isaiah 13): 'משא בבל,' 'משא מצרים,' 'משא מואב,' and when he came to mention this one, he said: 'משא דומה אלי קורא משעיר שמר מה מלילה' and so forth. And in another place he called it 'פריץ חיות' — that is what he said (Isaiah 35): 'לא יהיה שם אריה ופריץ חיות בל יעלנה לא תמצא שם והלכו גאולים.' And so we find Jeremiah the prophet mentioning all the kingdoms except this fourth kingdom — that is what he said (Jeremiah 5): 'על כן הכם אריה מיער זאב ערבות ישדדם נמר שקד על עריהם כל היוצא מהנה יטרף.' And you will not find in all the Torah anyone who mentions this beast by name except Moses our teacher, of blessed memory, and Asaph the psalmist, who mentioned it more explicitly, interpreting the name of the nation and the name of its city, which is Rome — that is what he said (Psalms 50): 'יכרסמנה חזיר מיער' — and if you examine closely, you will find 'עין' attached, and 'רמי' will remain for you. And because of this, when he mentioned here the tenth chieftain, 'Aluf Magdiel,' which alludes to Rome according to the Midrash of our Rabbis of blessed memory, he added an explanation and said: 'Aluf Eiram (עירם)' — whose letters are 'מיער' (from the forest), meaning: this Aluf Magdiel is 'חזיר מיער.' And the letters of עיר are after the manner of the verse (Numbers 24): 'והאביד שריד מעיר,' which is the city of Rome. And so Isaiah, of blessed memory, mentioned (Isaiah 25): 'כי שמת מעיר לגל קריה בצורה למפלה' — and this is said concerning Rome, and its letters also produce 'ירעם.' And concerning it David, of blessed memory, said (Psalms 18): 'וירעם בשמים ה׳ ועליון יתן קולו ברד וגחלי אש' — that the Holy One, blessed be He, is destined to destroy it. And so Daniel prophesied concerning it: 'עד די קטילת חיותא והובד גשמה ויהיבת ליקידת אשא.' And so we find Obadiah, who prophesied concerning the destruction of Edom with the agreement of the heavenly court — that is what they said in the Midrash: 'Why was חזון written by only two prophets, Isaiah and Obadiah? Rather, חזון ascends [to the numerical value of] 101: Isaiah cursed Edom in the heavenly court, and Obadiah judged Edom in the heavenly court.' And so they interpreted in the chapters of the Heikhalot: 'until a man says to his fellow, take Rome for a perutah, and he says to him, it is not wanted by me' — end quote. And furthermore you will find among these ten chieftains 'Aluf Alvah,' and in Chronicles it is written 'Aluf Aliah,' in order to mention the Name fully in both of them, alluding to those who speak deviation against HaShem, for falsehood is in their right hand, and they devise against HaShem words that are not so. And as it is written (Psalms 2): 'ורוזנים נוסדו יחד על ה׳ ועל משיחו.' And they said in Bereshit Rabbah: 'Before a king reigned in Israel, a king was established in Edom, as it is said: ואלה המלכים וגו׳.' This corresponds to what is written: 'נחלה מבוהלת בראשונה' — this is the kingdom of Esav, which came in haste before the kingdom of Israel; 'ואחריתה לא תבורך' (Obadiah 1:21): 'ועלו מושיעים בהר ציון' and so forth." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah. 'And these are the kings who reigned in the land of Edom.' This alludes to the worlds that the Holy One, blessed be He, created through the attribute of Judgment before He created this world, and before His kingdom was revealed in this world — this is the meaning of 'before a king reigned over the children of Israel.' From this it follows that He would build worlds and destroy them, until He created these and combined within them the attribute of Mercy. And I cannot explain, for the matters are deep, exalted, and hidden, standing at the summit of the world." "<b>And from the way of</b> the trustworthy Kabbalah, you must know that there is no difference between the verse of unification — which is the verse of Shema — and the verse of Timna; and there is no difference between the passage of 'And these are the kings' and the verse of 'And the name of his wife was Meheitabel,' and the verse (Exodus 14): 'ויסע מלאך האלהים' and the Ten Commandments — for it is all one matter and one binding." "<b>And you should</b> examine: these kings are eight, and with the woman Meheitabel they are nine. And one should wonder what reason there is to mention the name of the wife of this king more than for the other kings mentioned in the passage — and who would grant that we knew who this woman was, for we do not find her in all the Torah except in this place. And what benefit reaches us when Scripture mentions her name." "<b>And now</b> I will give you understanding and illuminate the matter, and I say first that the name of the first king is הדד and the name of the second is הדר, and both are from the language of הוד והדר — 'splendor and majesty' — which are attributes, and the meaning of הדד בן בדד is the 13 attributes that are rectified and drawn down from the 10 sefirot, and the discerning one will find it. And it is a well-known thing that the 13 attributes are the governance of this lower world, which is the world of בחינה (examination), and therefore he called it עוית (Awit), because in it is the evil inclination that perverts a person's path, and from there crooked judgment goes forth according to the thought of the seers. And it says: וימלוך תחתיו הדר ושם עירו פעו — 'and Hadar reigned in his stead, and the name of his city was Pau' — for from there the souls fly forth, and עירו (his city) is from the language of עיר וקדיש — 'a watcher and a holy one.' And the name of his wife is מהיטבאל (Mehetabel); she who receives from Him is called מהיטבאל, for she is the attribute of His goodness of El, concerning which it is said to Moses (Exodus 33): ״אני אעביר כל טובי על פניך״, and she is also called אל (El), and it is said of Moses (Numbers 12): ״בכל ביתי נאמן הוא״, for 'the good man' is ביתו — 'his house.' And when he said בת מטרד (daughter of Matred), he added to explain that regarding his wife whom he mentioned, you should not understand an actual woman, but rather from the language of measure, as in (I Kings): ״אלפים בת יכיל״. And he further explained and said that she is מטרדת המחשבה — 'the disturber of thought' — for from her came the error of the one about whom the masters of truth said that he cut down the plantings. And he turned back and further explained that she is a measure comprising mercy and judgment, and this is what is meant by בת מי זהב — 'daughter of Me-Zahav' — that is, a measure of mercy and judgment, for the Shekhinah, which is called 'daughter,' is comprised of the six extremities in which there is mercy and judgment. Furthermore, it is known from the wisdom of our holy tongue that the letter דל״ת interchanges with the letter תי״ו among the letters דטלנ״ת, and if so, the word מטרד is like מטרת, and it is the Aramaic translation of משמרת (watch/guard). And in the psalm (Psalm 121), ״אשא עיני״ — 'I lift my eyes' — guarding (שמירה) appears six times, corresponding to the six extremities. And up to here he speaks of the supernal ones; from here onward he speaks of the lower ones, who are the chiefs of Esau, as it is written after this: ״ואלה שמות אלופי עשו למשפחותם״. And may HaShem show us wonders from His Torah and illuminate the eyes of our intellect with the light of His apprehension, and merit us to the prophecy of Hosea, that the heart may be in the knowledge of Him like the dawn prepared for its going forth, and like a flowing spring, for thus the Torah is likened to a spring, as Solomon, peace be upon him, said (Song of Songs 4): ״מעין גנים באר מים חיים ונוזלים מן לבנון״. And so it is written concerning the future (Isaiah 12): ״ושאבתם מים בששון ממעיני הישועה״."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 36, :notes {1 "The citation 'חזון עולה ע\"א' (101) is rendered as in the source. Multiple Aramaic phrases from Daniel are preserved verbatim in Hebrew script per voice rules. The note about Psalms 50 reading 'חזיר מיער' reflects the Rabbinic tradition that reads Psalms 80:14 (יכרסמנה חזיר מיער) rather than the received יכרסמנה חזיר מיער, though the author cites Psalms 50."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:18:41.396232148Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}